# Invoke the system "lsof" utility from a centralized script in the test framework.
# This lets us add options that will speed up lsof on some systems (YDBTest#721).

# "-n" inhibits the conversion of network numbers to host names, and is the option that actually makes
# lsof fast. Without it, lsof does a reverse DNS lookup for the peer address of every socket it lists.
# An address with no PTR record whose authoritative side does not answer makes that lookup time out
# rather than fail, and lsof blocks for the whole timeout. Measured on one system: a single such
# address held by a browser cost 10.5 seconds, after which the resolver negative-caches the answer and
# the next run takes 0.42 seconds. That is why the slowness looks intermittent.
#
# "-n" replaces an earlier workaround which excluded "chrome" and "firefox" processes, since a browser
# is a likely holder of such a socket. Excluding them lost information: those processes' open files
# were not listed at all. With "-n" the exclusion is unnecessary, so it has been removed and the
# output is complete again, at no cost in time.
# No information is lost by "-n" itself, because the network number it prints is what the kernel
# holds; the host name it would otherwise print is derived from it.

# "-P" inhibits the conversion of port numbers to port names. Again nothing is lost, since the number
# is what the kernel holds. It also makes com/is_port_in_use.csh reliable: that script greps its lsof
# output for the port NUMBER, which cannot match when lsof renders a well known port by name.

# Additionally, only look for processes belonging to the current user as it was seen to also speed up the
# lsof noticeably (without -u took 3.2 seconds vs with -u took 0.42 seconds). Note this does mean
# processes belonging to other users are not listed.

# Need the "-a" after the "-u $USER" to ensure any condition that user specified in "$*" is ANDed with the condition
# and not ORed (which is the default). For example if "$lsof mumps.dat" was the caller invocation, then translating
# this to "lsof -u $USER mumps.dat" will list all open files by the current user OR all files opened for mumps.dat.
# Adding the "-a" in between will ensure it translates to "lsof -u $USER -a mumps.dat" which would mean we will only
# list all open files by the current user AND that relate to the file mumps.dat.

lsof -n -P -u $USER -a $*
